        tags$li(tags$b("CSV"), " (.csv): comma-separated values with a header row."),
        tags$li(tags$b("Excel"), " (.xlsx, .xls): first sheet is read by default; a sheet picker appears when multiple sheets are present."),
        tags$li(tags$b("Tab-delimited TXT"), " (.txt, .tsv, .tab): tab-separated values with a header row."),
        tags$li(tags$b("URL"), ": a publicly accessible Google Sheets or OneDrive share link.")
      ),
      h4("Wavelength increment / step (nm)"),
      p(strong("Wavelength spacing: "), "Any constant or near-constant step is accepted \u2014 for example 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, or 3 nm. The app detects your spacing automatically. You can optionally resample to a uniform grid under \u201cSignal conditioning \u2192 Resample to fixed grid\u201d; if your data are already evenly spaced you can leave resampling off."),
      h4("Example layout (wide format)"),
      helpText("nm in column 1, sample names in the header, absorbance values in the body:"),
      tableOutput("demo_format_preview"),
      tags$br(),
      downloadButton("dl_template", "Download blank template (CSV)", class="btn-sm btn-outline-primary"),
      tags$br(), tags$br(),
      h4("Notes"),
      tags$ul(
        tags$li("Wavelengths do not need to be in any particular order — the app auto-sorts them; duplicate wavelengths are averaged per sample."),
        tags$li("Missing cells are retained as missing and excluded or refused according to each operation's documented support rule."),
        tags$li("Long format (each row = one wavelength + one sample) is not currently supported; use wide format.")
      ),
      tags$hr(),
      h3("Advanced Mode & Spectral Deconvolution"),
      p("Advanced Mode is an optional toggle in the top navigation bar. Turning it ON adds ", tags$b("Deconvolution"), " between Data Quality and Sample color; turning it OFF removes that tab. If you are viewing Deconvolution when it is removed, the app moves to an available tab. Enabling Advanced Mode does not run a fit. It starts OFF for a fresh analysis; importing a session can restore the saved choice."),
      h4("What deconvolution does"),
      p("The Deconvolution tab represents the visible absorbance curve as a sum of ", tags$b("pseudo-Voigt components"), ". Each component blends Gaussian and Lorentzian profiles using one shape parameter shared across a fit. The tab reports each fitted component's center, FWHM, height, and area. This is a phenomenological partition of curve shape; it does not recover or identify pigments, transitions, or molecular contributions."),
      h4("How it differs from Band-AUC"),
      p("The ", tags$b("Band AUC"), " tab integrates the measured curve directly between fixed wavelength limits \u2014 it makes no assumption about peak shape and simply sums the area you bracket. ", tags$b("Deconvolution"), " instead models the curve as overlapping pseudo-Voigt peaks and assigns area to each fitted band. Band-AUC answers \u201chow much signal is in this window?\u201d; deconvolution answers \u201cif the curve were a sum of pseudo-Voigt peaks, how would the area split between them?\u201d The two are complementary, not interchangeable."),
      h4("Which curve is fitted"),
      p("Deconvolution preferentially fits processed analysis-curve values at native measured wavelength coordinates after the chosen analysis range, dilution scaling, baseline subtraction, and optional normalization. Display resampling and smoothing are not fit inputs. Requested normalization is re-evaluated on the effective fit curve; if it is not applied, the fit is refused and the reason/divisor are reported rather than labeling an unchanged curve as normalized. The provenance banner names the actual native-grid or documented fallback path used for the fit."),
      h4("The controls"),
      tags$ul(
        tags$li(tags$b("Sample"), ": which processed sample curve to fit."),
        tags$li(tags$b("Min / Max number of components"), ": the range of pseudo-Voigt peaks the fitter is allowed to try. The app fits each candidate count and selects the lowest chosen-criterion score among admissible candidates; it also reports the raw all-candidate minimum. If none is admissible, a diagnostic fit may be displayed but no K is selected."),
        tags$li(tags$b("Selection criterion (AIC-like / BIC-like / AICc-like plug-in score)"), ": the non-classical score used to rank admissible component counts — a ", tags$b("plug-in AR(1)-penalized parsimony score"), ", not a standard maximized-likelihood information criterion (the residual correlation ρ is plugged into a likelihood-style calculation, not jointly optimized). The BIC-like form usually penalizes extra peaks more strongly than the AIC-like form; the AICc-like correction can be undefined. Read selected K as a conditional parsimony result, not a molecular-species count."),
        tags$li(tags$b("Advanced settings"), " (peak-position tolerance, FWHM bounds, seed window): bounds and seeding hints for the non-linear fit. These are configurable development defaults, not validated limits; inspect sensitivity to them and report material changes.")
      ),
      h4("Interpretation notes"),
      p("Below the fitted-components table the tab shows an ", tags$b("Interpretation notes"), " panel generated by fixed rules from the fitted result. It flags selected numerical conditions such as small components, local uncertainty, score agreement, boundaries, and overlap. Absence of a flag means only that those rules did not fire; it does not validate the model or sample."),
      h4("Honesty caveat"),
      p(tags$b("Read this before interpreting results."), " Pseudo-Voigt deconvolution is a ", tags$em("phenomenological"), " curve-modeling technique, not a measurement of chemical species. A good fit means the curve can be ", tags$em("described"), " by that set of bands \u2014 it is ", tags$b("not"), " proof that those pigments or transitions are present, nor of their exact concentrations. Several different band sets can fit the same curve almost equally well. Treat the center wavelengths and areas as a quantitative description of the curve shape, corroborated against your own knowledge of the sample, not as identification or quantification on their own."),
      h4("Requirements"),
      p("Deconvolution requires baseline processing to be ON. The optional ", tags$code("minpack.lm"), " path can report local, model-conditional curvature-based standard errors when its covariance calculation is estimable; available values receive heuristic residual-correlation inflation and their coverage is not calibrated. If the package is absent, PASA uses an L-BFGS-B fallback without standard errors."))
    )
  ),
