                       "font-size:0.88em;line-height:1.4;"),
          tags$b("As-measured (instrument-blanked) OD. "),
          "PASA computes an apparent OD-derived log-linear rate (\u03bc) and OD doubling time on the uploaded as-measured basis. When using loaded spectra, an exact native node is used when present; otherwise a gap-guarded linear interpolation is used only when its native bracketing gap passes the archived coverage guard. The source sample, method, reason, bracket, gap, and guard are displayed/exported for every mapped timepoint. Calling \u03bc a biological specific growth rate requires an independently justified OD\u2013biomass relationship. ",
          "Provide OD that is already instrument-zeroed / medium-blanked (done on the spectrophotometer) \u2014 the app does not add a second blank. ",
          "What is intentionally bypassed is the ", tags$em("spectral-tail baseline"), " in Analysis settings (subtracting A at the reference \u03bb from every wavelength): at a 720\u2013750 nm tracking wavelength that would subtract the very turbidity signal you are tracking and flatten the exponential fit. ",
          "In manual-entry mode it uses the values you type verbatim. Keep OD within the instrument's linear range."),
      # The working area: dimmed/disabled until a path is active.
      uiOutput("growth_body")
    )
  ),
  nav_panel("References",
    div(class="justify-body", style="max-width:900px;margin:20px auto;",
      uiOutput("references_content")
    )
  ),
  # Help tab
  nav_panel("Help",
    div(class="justify-body", style="max-width:900px;margin:20px auto;",
      pasa_disclosure_actions("help_content"),
      uiOutput("help_content", class = "pasa-document-sections")
    )
  ),

  # FAQ tab
  nav_panel("FAQ",
    div(class="justify-body", style="max-width:860px;margin:20px auto;",
      h3(id = "pasa-faq-main", "Frequently Asked Questions"),
      div(style="margin:6px 0 14px;",
        tags$button("Expand all", type="button", class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary",
                    onclick="document.querySelectorAll('#faq_accordion details').forEach(function(d){d.open=true;});"),
        tags$button("Collapse all", type="button", class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary", style="margin-left:6px;",
                    onclick="document.querySelectorAll('#faq_accordion details').forEach(function(d){d.open=false;});")
      ),
      div(id="faq_accordion",
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("Who is this tool for, and why does it exist?")),
        p("Designed for researchers working with absorbance (UV-Vis) spectra of photosynthetic material across the full range of sample preparations: ",
          tags$b("purified pigment\u2013protein complexes"), ", ", tags$b("isolated thylakoid membranes"), ", ",
          tags$b("whole cells"), ", and ", tags$b("broken cells / lysate"), ". ",
          "The Protein-sample vs Cells / Lysate toggle and organism preset adapt the displayed wavelength-window labels; they do not identify pigments or molecular species."),
        p("It covers ", tags$b("cyanobacteria"), " (built around ", tags$em("Synechocystis"), " and similar systems), ",
          tags$b("red algae"), ", ", tags$b("green algae"), ", and ", tags$b("plants"),
          ". The initial defaults were chosen with cyanobacterial use cases in view but are configurable starting values, not validated organism-specific settings. Review wavelength windows and assumptions for each study."),
        p("The app provides baseline correction, band metrics (Qy / Soret / carotenoid / phycobilin / chlorophyll b), AUC, band ratios, ",
          "normalization, QC flagging, sample-similarity estimation, illustrative D65/CIE sRGB mapping, cell-growth kinetics, and export of processed data.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("Why do the band names change when I switch organism or sample type?")),
        p("The reported wavelength-window set is adaptive. The ", tags$b("Sample type"), " control (Protein sample · Isolated phycobiliprotein · Cells / Lysate, in the ",
          tags$em("Analysis range & pigment system"), " section) plus the organism preset determine which named windows are shown. ",
          "The 600–650 nm band is labeled “Chl a Qx / Vibronic Band” for protein samples; it becomes “PBS” (assembled phycobilisome) ",
          "for whole cells / lysate of cyanobacteria or red algae; and it becomes “Phycobiliprotein” for an ",
          tags$b("Isolated phycobiliprotein"), " (under that user-declared class, the 600–650 nm window is labeled “Phycobiliprotein” rather than PBS; PASA does not verify bilin absorption, identify the protein, or quantify it). ",
          "The Isolated-phycobiliprotein option is offered only for cyanobacteria and red algae; it does not auto-add the Phycoerythrin band and does not unlock the Cell Growth tab (whole cells only). ",
          "A separate Phycoerythrin (PE) band appears for red-algae cells, ",
          "and a Chl b Qy band appears for green algae and plants (which also renames the Qy band to “Chl a Qy”). ",
          "All ratios, OD values, plots and exports follow the active bands automatically.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("How does the Cell Growth tab work?")),
        p("It estimates an apparent OD-derived log-linear rate (μ) and OD doubling time of a single strain from an OD time series; calling this a biological specific growth rate requires an independently justified OD–biomass relationship. ",
          "It activates either when you select Cells / Lysate for a unicellular organism (then your activated samples are read at a ",
          "tracking wavelength of 720–750 nm; 730/750 nm are often used to reduce pigment interference but must be validated for the study), or when you tick the manual-entry checkbox to type OD values into a grid. ",
          "Manual entry is always available, independent of Sample type in Analysis settings. ",
          "Choose Days or Hours, any positive interval, and up to 10 replicates; intervals are assumed equidistant and only one strain is analyzed. ",
          "OD from loaded spectra uses the uploaded as-measured basis: an exact native node is used when present, otherwise a gap-guarded linear interpolation may be used only across an accepted native bracketing gap. The preview and observations CSV identify exact, interpolated, and unavailable values and archive the bracket/reason. The tab bypasses ", tags$em("Apply baseline correction"), " in Analysis settings (the spectral-tail step): at a 720\u2013750 nm tracking wavelength that per-spectrum subtraction would remove the very turbidity signal being tracked and flatten the exponential fit. This is not a claim that the numbers are \u2018blank-free\u2019 \u2014 the app adds no blank here, so your input must already be instrument-zeroed / medium-blanked (a constant medium blank biases \u03bc at low OD, so blank on the instrument first). ",
          "Click ", tags$b("Load built-in demo data"), " to populate a worked 3-replicate \u00d7 8-timepoint OD matrix and explore the tab without uploading anything. ",
          "Essential outputs (apparent OD-derived \u03bc, OD doubling time, growth curve, interpretation) show by default; advanced fits, phase boundaries, observations, the exact window-selection contract, and downloads are behind a checkbox. ",
          "A conditional sensitivity interval is shown with at least 3 fitted biological replicates; it is not a calibrated 95% confidence interval because auto-selected-window uncertainty is omitted.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("What file formats can I upload?")),
        p("CSV (.csv), Excel (.xlsx or .xls), and tab-delimited TXT (.txt, .tsv, .tab) are all accepted. ",
          "You can also paste a publicly accessible Google Sheets or OneDrive share link into the URL box. ",
          "Wide format (one column per sample) is required; see the ",
          tags$b("About"), " tab (Input data format section) for the exact layout.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("What wavelength spacing / increment is supported?")),
        p("Any constant or near-constant step is accepted \u2014 for example 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, or 3 nm. ",
          "The app detects your spacing automatically. You can optionally resample to a uniform grid under ",
          tags$em("Signal conditioning \u2192 Resample to fixed grid"),
          "; if your data are already evenly spaced you can leave resampling off.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("How do I format my input file?")),
        p("The first column must be wavelength in nm (column name like 'nm' or 'wavelength' is auto-detected). ",
          "Each subsequent column is one sample's absorbance values, with the sample name in the header row. ",
          "See the ", tags$b("About"), " tab (Input data format section) for a live example and a blank template download.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("Do I have to use my own data to try it?")),
        p("No. On the Input data tab, set the ", tags$b("Data source"), " toggle to ", tags$b("Demo data"), " and click ", tags$b("Load demo data"), " to instantly load a small synthetic dataset ",
          "(Ctrl, Sample A, Sample B). All app features work on it without uploading any file.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("What does baseline correction do?")),
        p("It subtracts an additive reference estimated from the full loaded source, which can include measured points outside the selected analysis range. ",
          "A far-red starting reference near 750 nm is common only when justified for the sample, solvent, blank, and instrument; ",
          "it removes one additive offset and does NOT make instruments directly comparable. Cross-instrument comparability also needs matched wavelength/bandwidth calibration, path length, blank, and scattering treatment."),
        p(tags$b("Single nm"), ": the entered wavelength is a request; PASA subtracts the value at the nearest eligible native measured node in the full loaded source. The requested and effective wavelengths can differ and the node can lie outside the analysis range. A common starting reference is near 750 nm, but it must be justified for the sample, solvent, blank, and instrument. A warning appears if the requested wavelength is below 730 nm."),
        p(tags$b("Window median baseline"), ": subtracts a robust estimate (the MEDIAN) of the absorbance across a short reference window, ",
          "computed only when at least three eligible native measured points occur in that requested window of the full loaded source. Those points may lie outside the analysis range. More robust to single-point noise than Single nm, but can ",
          "overcorrect if the window contains real pigment absorption or sits on a sloped scattering tail. A warning appears if the ",
          "lower bound is below 730 nm."),
        p("When baseline correction is ", tags$b("off"), ", the app shows the uploaded values before PASA baseline subtraction, preserving their input units and prior processing. ",
          "Values can be negative for source- or processing-specific reasons; PASA displays them without forcing them to zero. ",
          "Interpret negative values using the provenance of the imported data."),
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("When should I normalize, and what does it change?")),
        p("Normalize when you want to remove a uniform multiplicative scale for shape comparison. ",
          "That scale may reflect concentration, dilution, path length, matrix, instrument response, or several causes; ",
          "normalization does not identify the cause and sacrifices amplitude information."),
        p(tags$b("Baseline correction must be enabled before normalizing (conservative default)."),
          " Multiplicative normalization is offset-SENSITIVE: on raw OD that still carries an instrument baseline ",
          "offset, that offset enters the scaling factor and biases the result — so if you try to normalize while ",
          "baseline correction is off, the app warns and resets normalization to None. PASA has no separate ",
          "'already corrected' pipeline flag: prior processing remains part of the imported-data provenance. ",
          "To use PASA normalization, enable a justified PASA baseline operation and inspect its effect."),
        p("Normalization is applied after smoothing. This means the normalized band peak shown in the Metrics table ",
          "exactly equals the target value (e.g. Qy peak = 1.0000 when Band peak \u2192 target = 1 on Qy). ",
          "Pre-normalization metrics (OD at peak, band ratios, raw AUC) are unaffected by normalization.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("How are the Qy / Soret / carotenoid / phycobilin / chlorophyll b bands defined, and can I change them?")),
        p("The default band windows (e.g. Qy 660\u2013690 nm, Soret 420\u2013460 nm) are starting points based on typical ",
          "cyanobacterial spectra. They are fully adjustable using the sliders in Analysis settings. ",
          "The variable 3rd band spans 600\u2013650 nm; for cyanobacteria and red algae it is read as the phycobilisome (PBS) band for whole cells / lysate, ",
          "or as the \u201cPhycobiliprotein\u201d band for an isolated phycobiliprotein, and as the Chl a Qx / vibronic shoulder for a plain protein sample; ",
          "it switches to Chl b (640\u2013657 nm) for green algae and plants. ",
          "Use the ", tags$b("Organism / pigment system"), " selector in Analysis settings to apply the appropriate preset. ",
          "If your system has different peak positions, widen or shift the windows accordingly. ",
          "Band ratios are computed from whichever windows you set. These are starting points; ",
          "verify band positions with your own reference spectra.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("How does the sample-similarity estimate work, and is it definitive?")),
        p("The Data Quality tab computes three shape-based metrics for each sample pair that assess shape consistency ",
          "independent of overall amplitude: PASA's wavelength/trapezoid-weighted Pearson r on the pre-normalization analysis curves (endpoint half-weights mean it can differ from ordinary Pearson r even on a uniform finite grid; it saturates near 1 for pairs sharing the broad ",
          "envelope, but together with SAM it is a primary verdict driver), PASA's wavelength/trapezoid-weighted ",
          "spectral-angle variant (lower = more similar; conceptually scale-invariant to a common positive factor), and a wavelength/trapezoid-weighted Pearson r on the ",
          "second derivative (variable in development checks, so kept as an advisory diagnostic only). The four-tier verdict ",
          "is driven by weighted r plus weighted SAM on a pair-local union grid built from both native wavelength axes, with within-span linear interpolation and joint coverage/gap masking. ",
          "It highlights high-similarity pairs and unusual numerical relationships for provenance review; the metrics are described in detail in the Help tab."),
        p("These are estimation aids only, not a substitute for authenticated standards, chromatography/HPLC, mass spectrometry where applicable, or another study-specific independent assay. High similarity does not confirm biological identity; a difference does not identify the cause. Confirm interpretation with evidence appropriate to the study endpoint.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("Why are some \u201cpost-normalization\u201d columns hidden?")),
        p("Those columns appear only when a normalization method other than \u201cNone\u201d is selected in Analysis settings. ",
          "If you have not chosen a normalization method, the post-normalization columns are not shown.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("How do I export results?")),
        p("Each tab has its own download button(s). The Metrics tab offers XLSX and PDF exports, plus a ",
          tags$b("Copy the entire table"), " button that copies the full metrics data as tab-separated text to your clipboard ",
          "(paste directly into Excel or a spreadsheet application). ",
          "The Export options group in Analysis settings has pre- and, when normalization is active, post-normalization CSV/XLSX/TXT downloads. ",
          "Download everything (ZIP) in the header Export menu bundles the applicable tables, figures, reports and a full session snapshot ",
          "into one archive. Summary is the next tab after Analysis settings and offers a readable analysis record.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("What is the difference between the bar chart and radar chart in the Band AUC tab?")),
        p("The ", tags$b("bar chart"), " (default) shows one panel per sample with bars for each band, making it easy to see ",
          "the unnormalized or selected-basis AUC values for each window. Check ", tags$em("Use common Y-axis across samples"), " to make bar heights ",
          "visually comparable across panels (forced same y-limits). Uncheck it to use per-panel scaling, which is better ",
          "when samples differ greatly in signal magnitude, while remembering that free scales remove direct height comparability."),
        p("The ", tags$b("radar / spider chart"), " shows all bands as spokes of a polygon. It emphasizes the relative ",
          "proportions of each band, making it easy to spot changes in band balance. Toggle ",
          tags$em("Overlay all samples in one panel"), " to see all samples on one chart; uncheck for separate panels.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("Where did Probe at wavelength, Peaks, gridlines and band-range highlights go?")),
        p("Those plot-display controls are in the ", tags$b("Spectra"), " tab itself. ",
          "Open ", tags$b("Customize spectra plot"), " above the graph for overlay/facets, color palette, line style, line width, ",
          "intensity sliders and the four title fields. The controls are grouped into Curve style, Titles and axes, Wavelength probe, and Band peaks and labels. Open a group to inspect it; use its On/Off switch to enable a probe or band peaks. The 3D view has its own 3D rendering group. ",
          "The X- and Y-axis intensity sliders control the visibility of the axis lines themselves (the horizontal X-axis line and the vertical Y-axis line); tick labels and axis titles follow the active theme. ",
          "Enable the probe or band peaks inside that disclosure to show the corresponding marks and result tables below the graph. Numerical band windows remain in Analysis settings.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("How do I save and reload my settings and data?")),
        p("The ", tags$b("Session snapshot"), " block (in Analysis settings, mirrored on the Summary tab) has two synced checkboxes \u2014 ",
          tags$b("Settings"), " and ", tags$b("Input data"),
          ". Click ", tags$b("Save session"), " in the header to write a single ", tags$code("PASA_state.txt"),
          " file. Input data also carries Cell Growth entries/mappings and an explicit measured-blank section. ",
          tags$b("Download everything (ZIP)"), " always embeds the full snapshot, even when a standalone-export checkbox is off. It preserves the parsed active wide table and PASA state, not necessarily the exact original uploaded byte stream. Settings are stored as machine-parseable ",
          tags$code("key=value"), " lines so they round-trip back to the UI exactly. Reload by setting the ", tags$b("Data source"), " toggle to ", tags$b("Import session"),
          " on the Input data tab; the dialog only shows sections that are actually present in the file.")
      ),
      tags$details(id="pasa-faq-settings-location",
        tags$summary(tags$b("Where are the input and analysis settings now?")),
        p("Open PASA starts in ", tags$b("Input data"), ", where you load a file, reopen a session or use the built-in example. ",
          "The next tab, ", tags$b("Analysis settings"), ", contains range and sample type, Samples, Band windows, Baseline, Signal conditioning, Scaling and normalization, and Export options. ",
          "The settings stay in their own tab instead of appearing above every page. Summary follows immediately after it."),
        p("The top-right Analysis settings shortcut returns here from analysis pages. It is hidden on Welcome, About, Help, FAQ, References and Feedback. Expand all and Collapse all let you open or fold the settings groups.")),
      tags$details(id="pasa-faq-guided-tours",
        tags$summary(tags$b("Which guided tour should I choose, and can I resume it?")),
        p(tags$b("Quick"), " introduces the workflow, every tab and Advanced Mode. ", tags$b("Detailed"), " explains the individual options, outputs and examples, with an especially thorough Analysis settings chapter. ",
          "Open Guided tour from the header or Welcome, or use Detailed guided tour inside Analysis settings to start that chapter directly."),
        p("Jump to chapter moves between subjects. Next field highlights related controls one by one. Pause tour or Escape retains the current position, and the chooser offers separate Resume buttons for the two tours. ",
          "When a field needs another option or data, the tour explains how to reveal it; you can continue and use Revisit later. The tour does not change analysis settings, replace data, run jobs, download results or send feedback.")),
      tags$details(id="pasa-faq-wide-metrics",
        tags$summary(tags$b("How can I reach distant columns in a wide Metrics table?")),
        p("Use the synchronized horizontal scrollbars at the top or bottom of the table, or the translucent arrows at its sides. ",
          "This lets you reach later sample columns while reading any part of a long table. Each separate band table has independent controls. Scrolling does not remove columns from the complete table, Copy the entire table, or its downloads.")),
      tags$details(id="pasa-faq-color-scene",
        tags$summary(tags$b("What do the cuvette scene and Color details represent?")),
        p("Sample color places the illustrative liquid colors into a static photographic lab-bench scene with transparent cuvettes and a light source, without an animated beam, filter, detector or spectrum screen. ",
          "Choose one sample or Show all sample colors. Selected samples wrap into rows to fit the view; for example, fifteen samples can appear in three rows of five on a wide view. Open the Color details arrow below the image for full names, hexadecimal codes and rendering information; the table is folded by default."),
        p("Scene background offers Dark (the original blue-gray laboratory) and Light (white-painted bench and walls with the same texture). It is independent of the app theme, is saved with session settings, and applies to image exports. It does not change the calculated hexadecimal colors."),
        p("The hexadecimal code in Color details gives the exact calculated sRGB color; the shaded glass and lighting are an illustration. ",
          "The D65/CIE mapping, reference scaling, weak-signal safeguards and declared wavelength-coverage limits still apply. PASA has no path-length input and this is not a calibrated prediction of a physical sample appearance.")),
      tags$details(id="pasa-faq-feedback",
        tags$summary(tags$b("How do I provide feedback, including from an offline computer?")),
        p("Open ", tags$b("Feedback"), " after References, or choose Feedback in Contacts immediately below LinkedIn. Select a reaction and add an optional comment. ",
          "Send feedback uses PASA's existing Google Form without asking for your name, email address, or sign-in. Select a reaction, optionally write a comment, and acknowledge the notice before sending. Google can receive normal request metadata. Spectra and analysis results are not attached automatically."),
        p("Sending needs an internet connection. A failed or unconfirmed send keeps your draft; Save feedback (.txt) makes a local copy to transfer and send later. Compose email is an optional alternative that reveals the sender address used in your email app. An operator can disable direct sending or configure another service. ",
          "The Feedback tab remains available through email and text even when direct submission is not configured. Include the affected tab, expected behavior and steps to reproduce a problem.")),
      h4(id="pasa-faq-advanced", "Advanced Mode & Deconvolution", style="margin-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--bs-border-color);padding-top:12px;"),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("What is Advanced Mode and the Deconvolution tab?")),
        p(tags$b("Advanced Mode"), " is the switch in the top navigation bar. Turn it ON to reveal ", tags$b("Deconvolution"), " between Data Quality and Sample color; turn it OFF to remove that tab. If Deconvolution is the active view when it is removed, an available tab is selected. Enabling the mode does not run a fit. It starts OFF for a fresh analysis; a saved session can restore its earlier choice."),
        p("The ", tags$b("Deconvolution"), " tab represents the processed absorbance curve as a sum of ", tags$b("pseudo-Voigt components"), " sharing one global Gaussian-Lorentzian shape parameter, and reports each fitted component's center, FWHM, height, and area. This partitions the modeled curve phenomenologically; it does not recover or identify pigments, transitions, or molecular contributions.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("How is deconvolution different from Band-AUC?")),
        p(tags$b("Band AUC"), " integrates the measured curve directly between fixed wavelength limits and makes no assumption about peak shape \u2014 it answers \u201chow much signal is in this window?\u201d ", tags$b("Deconvolution"), " models the curve as overlapping pseudo-Voigt peaks and assigns area to each fitted band \u2014 it answers \u201cif the curve were a sum of pseudo-Voigt bands, how would the area split between them?\u201d They are complementary, not interchangeable.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("Which curve does deconvolution actually fit \u2014 and does normalization affect it?")),
        p("It preferentially fits processed analysis-curve values at native measured wavelength coordinates after your analysis range, dilution scaling, baseline subtraction, and ", tags$b("optional normalization"), ". Display resampling and smoothing are not fit inputs. A requested normalization is fitted only when its effective native-fit status is applied; otherwise Run is refused with the archived reason and divisor. The provenance banner reports that effective state, so the control request cannot silently relabel an unchanged curve.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("Can I trust the fitted bands as real pigments or concentrations?")),
        p(tags$b("No \u2014 read this carefully."), " Pseudo-Voigt deconvolution is a ", tags$em("phenomenological"), " curve-modeling technique, not a measurement of chemical species. A good fit means the curve ", tags$em("can be described"), " by that set of bands; it is ", tags$b("not"), " proof that those pigments or transitions are present, nor of their concentrations, and several different band sets can fit the same curve almost equally well. Treat the fitted centers and areas as a quantitative description of curve shape to corroborate against orthogonal evidence (HPLC, known standards, your knowledge of the sample), not as standalone identification or quantification.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("How does the app choose the number of components, and what if the AIC-like and BIC-like scores disagree?")),
        p("The fitter tries every component count between your Min and Max settings and scores each with the selected AIC-like, BIC-like, or AICc-like plug-in AR(1)-penalized parsimony formula. Selected K is the lowest score among admissible candidates. These are not classical likelihood-based information criteria. The raw all-candidate minimum is reported separately; if no candidate is admissible, a diagnostic fit may be shown but no K is selected."),
        p("The ", tags$b("Interpretation notes"), " panel compares guard-adjusted AIC-like and BIC-like selections within the same plug-in scoring family and discloses their raw minima. Agreement is conditional on this data, model family, search, bounds, preprocessing, and admissibility rules; it does not establish a true component count or general robustness."),
        p(tags$b("Important caveat — the lag-1 residual ρ."), " The plug-in scores use a correlated-residual model because serial correlation may arise from lineshape mismatch, correlated acquisition or preprocessing noise, sampling, or a combination of these. A large |ρ| makes an i.i.d. residual assumption questionable but does not diagnose the physical cause. PASA estimates ρ after each curve fit and folds it into a plug-in AR(1)-penalized score on native-grid analysis residuals; this is not a joint maximum-likelihood AR(1) fit, and ordinary BIC regularity does not strictly hold when a component enters at zero amplitude. Treat “Selected K” as a reproducible ", tags$em("parsimony heuristic"), " rather than a count of molecular species; inspect residuals, sensitivity to preprocessing and bounds, and bootstrap K-stability.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("What is the \u201cSearch-depth limit\u201d toggle (Tested range / Advanced opt-in)?")),
        p("It sets the ceiling on the ", tags$b("k_max"), " field \u2014 how deep the component-count (K) sweep is allowed to search. You can still edit k_max freely within the cap:"),
        tags$ul(
          tags$li(tags$b("Tested range (green) \u2014 caps K at 4."), " This default matches the component-count range exercised by the separately distributed synthetic recovery study. It does not validate any fitted K or identify a molecular species."),
          tags$li(tags$b("Advanced opt-in (red) \u2014 caps K at 20."), " Every K > 4 is outside the separately distributed recovery study. Use the wider range only as an explicitly reported sensitivity analysis; expect greater runtime, non-identifiability, and convergence risk.")
        ),
        p("The permanent advisory box under the controls states whether the live k_max is inside or outside the tested K = 1-4 range (Meloun et al., 2000).")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("Why do the standard errors show as \u201c\u2014\u201d in the components table?")),
        p("Only the ", tags$b("nlsLM"), " path \u2014 the bounded Levenberg\u2013Marquardt fit from the optional ", tags$code("minpack.lm"), " package \u2014 can supply local, model-conditional covariance-based standard errors. Convergence does not guarantee that the covariance is estimable, so some or all SE cells can still show \u201c\u2014\u201d. If ", tags$code("minpack.lm"), " is not installed, or the LM fit fails to converge on your curve, the app falls back to an ", tags$b("L-BFGS-B"), " optimizer without standard errors. The method badge above the plot tells you which path ran and whether finite SEs are shown.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("The interpretation notes flagged a tiny or near-noise component \u2014 should I drop it?")),
        p("A component is flagged when its amplitude is below 3\u00d7 the fit\u2019s residual RMS, or as a softer guideline when its area is under ~2% of the modeled total. Both are operational heuristics, not validated detection or concentration limits. A flagged component may be ", tags$b("mathematical rather than molecular"), ". It is not automatically wrong \u2014 but treat it sceptically, check whether a smaller K fits nearly as well, and retain a molecular interpretation only with independent evidence.")
      ),
      tags$details(
        tags$summary(tags$b("Why is the Deconvolution tab disabled or asking me to enable something?")),
        p("Deconvolution requires baseline processing to be ON. The optional ", tags$code("minpack.lm"), " path can report local, model-conditional curvature-based standard errors when its covariance calculation is estimable; available values receive heuristic residual-correlation inflation and their coverage is not calibrated. Without it, PASA uses an L-BFGS-B fallback and reports no standard errors.")
      )
      ) # end faq_accordion
    )
  ),
  nav_panel("Feedback", value="Feedback",
    div(id="pasa-feedback-panel", class="emoji-survey", style="max-width:760px;margin:24px auto;padding:0 16px;",
      h3("Help improve PASA"),
      p("Share an idea, report a problem, or tell us what worked well. Your feedback helps shape the next version."),
